  "question": {
    "text": "Three rods of identical cross-sectional area and made from the same metal, form the sides of an isosceles triangle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>C</mi></math>&nbsp;right angled at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>B</mi></math> as shown in figure. The point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>A</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>B</mi></math> are maintained at temperature <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>T</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mi>T</mi></math> respectively, in the&nbsp;steady state. Now, assuming that only heat conduction takes place. The temperature of point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>C</mi></math> will be<br /><img src=\"assets\\qt0004_78046426a38d.png\" style=\"width: 200px; height: 188px;\" />"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mi>T</mi><mrow><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mi>T</mi><mrow><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>T</mi></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></math>",
      "is_correct": true
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt><mi>T</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow></mfrac></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    }
  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "C"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></math> be the temperature of point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>C</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>x</mi></math> be the length of rod&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi></math> or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>B</mi><mi>C</mi></math>.</p><p>Then, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>C</mi><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><msqrt><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></msqrt><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mi>x</mi></math></p><p>At steady state, the rate of heat flowing from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>B</mi></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo></math> rate of heat flowing from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>C</mi></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>A</mi></math>.</p><p>So, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>K</mi><mi>A</mi><mfenced><mrow><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mi>T</mi><mo>-</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mfenced></mrow><mi>x</mi></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>K</mi><mi>A</mi><mfenced><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>-</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mfenced></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mi>x</mi></mrow></mfrac></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mfenced><mrow><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mi>T</mi><mo>-</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mfenced><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>-</mo><mi>T</mi></math></p><p>By solving, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>T</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow></mfrac></math></p>"
  }
